course: euphemisms
lesson: 31
title: Soft words for hard things: drinking and drugs and religion and fate
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: People often feel awkward talking about drinking too much alcohol or taking drugs, so they use soft or funny words instead: a famous person's office may say they are in hospital with "exhaustion" when the real trouble was drink or drugs.
explain: Some of these words are kind (they protect a person who is ill with addiction), some hide the truth, and some are jokes that make heavy drinking sound like harmless fun.
explain: For hundreds of years people also avoided saying the names of God, the Devil and hell out loud, because it felt rude or unlucky, so they said "the man upstairs", "old Nick" or "down below" instead.
explain: Bad luck gets soft words too: "fate had other plans" is a gentle way of saying that something simply did not happen or went wrong.
explain: A good reader asks: who is speaking, and are they being kind, polite, joking, or hiding something?
fact: "One for the road" means one last alcoholic drink before leaving.
fact: "Went into treatment" means a person went to a clinic (rehab) to get help with an addiction.
fact: "Exhaustion", in a press statement about a famous person, often hides a crisis with drink or drugs.
fact: "For medicinal purposes" is a joke meaning for drinking or getting high, not really for health.
fact: "The drink", said in Ireland as in "he had a problem with the drink", means alcoholism, being unable to stop drinking alcohol.
fact: "A session", in Ireland and Britain, means a long bout of heavy drinking.
fact: "A skinful" (UK slang) means enough alcohol to make someone very drunk.
fact: "Tied one on" (US slang) means got drunk.
fact: "Wobbly pops" (UK, humorous) means alcoholic drinks.
fact: "The man upstairs" means God.
fact: "The big guy upstairs" (US, humorous) means God.
fact: "The good Lord willing" means if God allows it, that is, if nothing goes wrong.
fact: "An act of God", in insurance (paying a company so that it pays you if your things are damaged) and contracts, means a natural disaster such as a flood or storm that nobody can be blamed or made to pay for.
fact: "The other place" (old-fashioned, UK) means hell.
fact: "Down below" (old-fashioned) means hell.
fact: "Old Nick" (old-fashioned, UK) means the Devil.
fact: "Old Scratch" (old-fashioned, US) means the Devil.
fact: "The deuce", in old exclamations like "what the deuce?", means the Devil.
fact: "The dickens", in exclamations like "what the dickens is that?", means the Devil, not the writer Charles Dickens.
fact: "Fate had other plans" means that something did not happen or went wrong.
exercise: What does "one for the road" mean when a friend says it at the pub? | One last drink before leaving | A snack to eat on the drive home | Money for the bus fare | A song to sing on the way
exercise: What does "went into treatment" usually mean in news about a singer? | Went to a clinic for help with addiction | Had a beauty treatment at a spa | Was treated to a free holiday | Had a small operation on a sore knee in hospital
exercise: A film star's office says she is in hospital with "exhaustion". What may it really be hiding? | A crisis with drink or drugs | That she worked too many hours | That she has a bad cold | That she broke her leg
exercise: Grandad pours a whisky "for medicinal purposes" with a wink. What does he really mean? | He just wants a drink | His doctor told him to drink it | It will cure his cough | It is a kind of medicine
exercise: What does "he had a problem with the drink" mean in Ireland? | He was an alcoholic | He spilled a lot of drinks | He did not like drinking water | He was thirsty all the time
exercise: What does "a session" mean when an Irish uncle says "we had a great session last night"? | A long night of heavy drinking | A long school lesson | A long meeting at the office about work | A visit to the doctor
exercise: What does "he'd had a skinful" mean in Britain? | He was very drunk | He had eaten too much | He had a bad sunburn | He was very cold
exercise: What does "tied one on" mean in American slang? | Got very drunk | Tied up a shoelace | Put on a necktie | Tied up a boat
exercise: What are "wobbly pops" in British jokey talk? | Alcoholic drinks | Jelly sweets | Fizzy lemonade | Balloons that pop
exercise: Who is "the man upstairs" when someone says "only the man upstairs knows"? | God in heaven | The neighbour in the flat above | The head teacher | The landlord
exercise: Who is "the big guy upstairs" in American jokey talk? | God, up in heaven | A tall neighbour | The boss of the company | A giant in a story
exercise: What does "we'll be there Friday, the good Lord willing" mean? | We'll be there if nothing goes wrong | We'll be there only if a priest comes | We'll be there and pray on Friday | We'll be there only if the boss agrees
exercise: What is "an act of God" in an insurance letter? | A natural disaster nobody can be blamed for | A miracle that saves the house from falling down | A prayer said at church | A good deed done by a stranger
exercise: What is "the other place" when an old preacher says bad people go there? | The fires of hell | The other side of town | Another church | A prison
exercise: What is "down below" in an old story about sinners? | Hell, under the earth | The cellar of the house | The bottom of the sea | The lower deck of a ship
exercise: Who is "Old Nick" in an old British tale? | The Devil himself | Father Christmas | A grumpy old farmer | A thief called Nicholas
exercise: Who is "Old Scratch" in an old American story? | The Devil himself | A cat that scratches | A rusty old car | A grumpy old dog
exercise: What does "what the deuce is going on?" mean? | What the devil is going on? | What the second thing is going on? | What the two of them are doing? | What the time is now?
exercise: "What the dickens is that noise?" Who is "the dickens"? | The Devil himself | The writer Charles Dickens | A naughty little boy | A kind of bird
exercise: What does "we planned a picnic but fate had other plans" mean? | The picnic did not happen | The picnic was moved to a better place | A man called Fate came along | The picnic was planned again by someone else
test: News: "The footballer has gone into treatment and asks for privacy." What has really happened? | He is getting help for an addiction | He has hurt his leg badly in training on Saturday | He is on holiday with his family | He has started a new job
test: "Sorry I'm late, Dad had one for the road and then couldn't find his keys." What did Dad do? | He had one last drink before leaving | He ate a sandwich on the way | He drove one more lap of the town | He bought a road map
test: Gossip: "Poor Mrs Kelly, her husband's fond of the drink." What is she worried about? | Her husband drinks too much alcohol | Her husband drinks too much tea | Her husband is always thirsty | Her husband sells drinks
test: "Bloody hell, look at him, he's had a right skinful." What is the speaker saying? | The man is very drunk | The man is very fat | The man is very sunburnt | The man is very angry
test: A press release: "The actor cancelled the tour due to exhaustion." What might a careful reader suspect? | Trouble with drink or drugs | He wanted a longer holiday | The tickets did not sell | The weather was too hot
test: "we tied one on last nite lol head is killing me" What happened last night? | They got drunk | They went sailing | They wore fancy ties | They tied up a parcel
test: Insurance letter: "We cannot pay for the damage, as the flood was an act of God." What does the company mean? | It was a natural disaster nobody is to blame for | A priest caused the flood | God will pay for the damage instead of the company | The flood was a miracle
test: Nan says: "We'll see you at Christmas, the good Lord willing." What does she mean? | She will come if nothing goes wrong | She will come only if she prays first | She will not come this year | She will come with the vicar
test: "what the dickens have u done to the kitchen?!" How is the speaker feeling? | Shocked and cross | Happy and proud | Sleepy and bored | Calm and pleased
test: A cartoon villain shouts "Go to the other place!" Where is he telling them to go? | To hell | To another town | To the next room | To the other team
test: "wobbly pops for the grown-ups, juice for the kids" — what will the grown-ups drink? | Alcoholic drinks | Wobbly jelly | Fizzy water | Ice lollies
test: Mia says: "Old Nick means Father Christmas, right? St Nicholas!" Is she right? | No, Old Nick means the Devil | Yes, it is Father Christmas | Yes, it means any old man | No, it means a thief
test: "The holiday was all booked, but fate had other plans — Dad broke his leg." What happened to the holiday? | It did not happen | It was even better | It was moved to the seaside | It was paid for twice
exam: Pursuant to the insurer's correspondence, the destruction of the dwelling is attributed to "an act of God". Identify the meaning of this expression. | A natural disaster for which no person can be held liable | A deliberate act committed by a member of the clergy | A miraculous event resulting in the preservation of property | An act of vandalism committed by persons unknown
exam: Whereas a public statement asserts that a performer has "gone into treatment", which of the following most accurately characterises the situation? | The performer is receiving clinical assistance for an addiction | The performer has undergone a cosmetic procedure | The performer has been granted a period of paid leave | The performer has been admitted with a fractured limb
exam: Ascertain the referent of the expression "Old Nick" as employed in traditional British narrative. | The Devil | Saint Nicholas, otherwise known as Father Christmas | An elderly agricultural labourer | A notorious highwayman of the eighteenth century
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the phrase "for medicinal purposes" when uttered jocularly in respect of an alcoholic beverage. | The beverage is consumed for pleasure rather than for health | The beverage has been prescribed by a physician | The beverage possesses proven curative properties | The beverage is intended exclusively for the treatment of a cough
